/**
 * CONSTRAINT IDENTIFICATION
 *   constraint_id: simultaneous_veneration__ontological_fusion_reading
 *   human_readable: Honji-Suijaku Ontological Fusion: Buddhist Interpretive Monopoly over Kami
 *   domain: religious/metaphysical/institutional
 *
 * SUMMARY:
 *   In medieval and early modern Japan, simultaneous veneration of kami
 *   (indigenous deities rooted in place, history, and agricultural cycles)
 *   and buddhist figures (buddhas and bodhisattvas claimed as universal
 *   salvific truth) faced a logical coherence problem. Honji-suijaku theory
 *   (the original essence / manifest trace distinction) solved this by
 *   asserting kami are local manifestations of universal Buddhist entities —
 *   a single metaphysical reality expressed through different cultural
 *   lenses. This constraint story instantiates ONE reading of a contested
 *   kernel: the simultaneous-veneration fact. The honji-suijaku reading
 *   claims to capture metaphysical truth; two sibling readings
 *   (domain-partition reading: kami and buddhas are genuinely distinct;
 *   pragmatic-incoherence reading: coherence was never achieved) offer
 *   structurally different accounts of the same historical practice. This
 *   story traces honji-suijaku's structural consequences: Buddhist
 *   institutional hierarchy claims interpretive monopoly; indigenous kami
 *   practitioners' autonomy narratives are subordinated; local shrine
 *   authority is absorbed into Buddhist temple networks. The authoring
 *   position separates the truth claim from the extraction claim (independent
 *   axes): a doctrine can be metaphysically true AND extractive if only
 *   certain institutional actors are permitted to interpret it. This story
 *   traces the extractive institutional path generated by the
 *   ontological-fusion reading.
 *
 * KEY AGENTS:
 *   - buddhist_institutional_hierarchy: Institutional agenda-setter and beneficiary; controls temple networks, textual transmission, ordination; benefits from honji-suijaku's elevation of Buddhist cosmology as universal explanatory framework; power: institutional; exit: arbitrage (can adopt alternatives at prestige cost).
 *   - indigenous_kami_practitioners: Primary victim; identity-locked (kami veneration constitutive of local identity and place); bear cost of interpretive subordination — their own account of kami as autonomous beings is reframed as metaphysically mistaken; power: moderate; exit: identity-locked (cannot exit without losing cultural self).
 *   - local_shrine_autonomy: Institutional victim; loses independent authority when kami redefined as Buddha-manifestations; absorbed into Buddhist temple network; power: moderate; exit: constrained (refusal means marginalization).
 *   - buddhist_scholars_theologians: Beneficiaries; advance careers through mastery of honji-suijaku framework; power: institutional; exit: mobile (can adopt alternatives but lose prestige).
 *   - lay_practitioners: Asymmetric beneficiary-payer; experience genuine intellectual satisfaction (unified cosmology) but diminished interpretation authority (defer to institutional Buddhist reading); power: powerless; exit: constrained.
 *   - alternative_reading_advocates: Excluded (would argue domain-partition or pragmatic-incoherence); barred from institutional authority; power: powerless; exit: trapped.
 *   - meiji_state_authorities: Analytical observers; enforce separation (1868+), revealing prior constraint was maintained by institutional authority, not metaphysical necessity.
 */

